WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.048
The method has three pieces.

00:00:04.128 --> 00:00:08.176
First, a memory function for nonlinear functionals:
it measures,

00:00:08.256 --> 00:00:12.303
over Heaviside step inputs,

00:00:12.383 --> 00:00:16.431
how strongly the output at time t still depends on
the input, giving a precise,

00:00:16.511 --> 00:00:20.559
task-independent, and numerically queryable notion
of memory.

00:00:20.639 --> 00:00:24.687
Second,

00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:28.814
a stability framework: the perturbation error at
hidden dimension m is the worst-case

00:00:28.894 --> 00:00:32.942
output error when parameters are perturbed within a
ball of radius beta,

00:00:33.022 --> 00:00:37.070
and a target is beta-zero stably approximated if
that error stays continuous up to some positive radius.

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:41.198
The central theorem then says: if a target with
decaying memory is stably approximated

00:00:41.278 --> 00:00:45.326
by RNNs whose weight norms and perturbed memories
stay uniformly controlled,

00:00:45.406 --> 00:00:49.453
then the target's memory must decay exponentially.

00:00:49.533 --> 00:00:53.581
The proof leverages stability to drive hidden-state
derivatives to zero and uses the Hartman-Grobman

00:00:53.661 --> 00:00:57.709
theorem to bound the recurrent eigenvalues.

00:00:57.789 --> 00:01:01.837
Finally, to overcome this curse,

00:01:01.917 --> 00:01:05.964
they substitute the recurrent weight matrix with a
continuous function that always maps into stable,

00:01:06.044 --> 00:01:10.092
negative-real-part matrices, such as an exponential
or softplus map,

00:01:10.172 --> 00:01:14.220
so the network can push eigenvalues toward zero
while remaining stable under perturbation.
